		<content type="html" xml:base="http://localhost/opengd77/viewtopic.php?t=3708&amp;p=28945#p28945"><![CDATA[
<blockquote class="uncited"><div><blockquote class="uncited"><div>The only bugs I currently know about are<br><br>1. Problem with terrible audio quality on a Non-GPS radio.  This problem may not be related to whether the radio is Non-GPS.<br>Please let me know if you installed on a Non-GPS version<br>2. Speaker clicks<br>3. Loose battery connection caused radio to hang<br>4. Orange button does not seem to work perfectly for everyone.  I received 1 report of the Orange button opening the Quick Menu but not closing it.<br>5. Problem of occasional speaker click when no DMR signal. This bug is not specific to the MD2017/RT-82, it also occurs on the DM1701 and may occur on the UV380</div></blockquote>I have a Non-GPS MD-2017 and can report the following: <br><br>1. Audio is fine, no issues here<br>2. Speaker clicks, yes, I have those, but it's not really annoying<br>3. No loose battery here, so no issues with this<br>4. My orange button is hard to push (and has always been), but otherwise it works well. <br>5. Radio open for a couple of hours besides me on my desk and no issues with spurios speaker clicks. <br><br>I hope this info is useful! <br><br>73 de EA7KLK</div></blockquote>Thanks<br><br>Speaker clicks when playing beeps etc are also in the official firmware, so this appears to be a fault in the electronics design of this radio.<br>I will investigate whether there is anything I can do in the firmware to reduce them, perhaps using the same method of "click suppression" which I used for the MD9600, but at the moment I don't know if that will be possible on the MD2017<br><br><br>The supurious speaker clicks are only on DMR and occur when the Green LED illuminates / flashes occasionally when there is no DMR signal.<br>People reported the same problem on the DM-1701 but I didn't have time to test this.<br><br><br>BTW.<br><br>For me DMR audio is a lot quieter than FM audio, so I will investigate whether I can change the FM audio gain to reduce this imbalance<p>Statistics: Posted by <a href="http://localhost/opengd77/memberlist.php?mode=viewprofile&amp;u=50">VK3KYY</a> — Thu Jan 25, 2024 6:57 am</p><hr />
]]></content>
